WEBVTT

00:00:00.035 --> 00:00:04.485
On Thursday the 19th of November,
Britain called in sick. Not a few people.

00:00:04.525 --> 00:00:08.750
Not a cheeky Friday. The alarm went
off and half the country looked at it,

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:11.838
looked at the telly, and made
a decision: I'm not going in.

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:15.839
Grand Theft Auto VI had unlocked
at midnight. After thirteen years.

00:00:15.879 --> 00:00:19.441
After two delays. After a wait so
long some of you went from schoolkids

00:00:19.481 --> 00:00:21.027
to mortgage holders in the gap.

00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:25.088
And on Thursday morning something very
British happened. People didn't riot.

00:00:25.128 --> 00:00:28.046
They didn't camp outside the
shops. They just didn't show up.

00:00:28.086 --> 00:00:30.870
Stay with me, because the sick
days are only the opening.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:33.983
The strange part comes later:
a Travelodge, a Fire Stick,

00:00:34.023 --> 00:00:37.594
and a man who walked out of court the
same week the country clocked off.

00:00:37.634 --> 00:00:41.451
I promise I'll come back to that. First,
I need you to picture that Thursday.

00:00:41.491 --> 00:00:44.738
An HR company called BrightHR
looks after more than a million

00:00:44.778 --> 00:00:47.656
workers at about 70,000 British firms.

00:00:47.696 --> 00:00:52.266
On that morning, sickness absence on
their books jumped 56% on the week before.

00:00:52.306 --> 00:00:53.206
56%.

00:00:53.668 --> 00:00:56.601
Another firm, Deel, said leave
requests for the day were

00:00:56.641 --> 00:01:01.256
up 21% on the same Thursday last year.
Now, that isn't a government census.

00:01:01.296 --> 00:01:05.052
It's what those companies could see. But
you and I both know what it looks like.

00:01:05.092 --> 00:01:08.778
It looks like a nation that had been
waiting thirteen years for a Thursday.

00:01:08.818 --> 00:01:12.425
A poll taken just before
launch found around 2.8 million

00:01:12.465 --> 00:01:14.880
UK adults planned to play on day one.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:18.731
One in twenty adults shaping their
whole Thursday around a video game.

00:01:18.771 --> 00:01:22.941
Some did it properly: annual leave
booked weeks out, the honest approach.

00:01:22.981 --> 00:01:25.710
Some were suddenly,
tragically, very unwell.

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:28.633
And if you were a boss that
morning, staring at an empty

00:01:28.673 --> 00:01:30.419
desk, you probably weren't laughing.

00:01:30.459 --> 00:01:34.550
You'd have rotas to fill, calls to make,
a Thursday that still needed doing.

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:36.446
But if you were one of the
people who'd finally got

00:01:36.486 --> 00:01:40.122
their hands on it, you weren't
skiving. You were enjoying your life.

00:01:40.162 --> 00:01:42.173
Because here's what a lot
of the coverage missed:

00:01:42.213 --> 00:01:44.338
this wasn't just people
hiding under a duvet.

00:01:44.378 --> 00:01:49.152
This was a country giving itself permission.
Sam from St Albans didn't mess about.

00:01:49.192 --> 00:01:53.178
He booked four days off, spent
nearly £1,500 on a new set-up:

00:01:53.218 --> 00:01:55.996
new telly, custom
controller, the full bunker.

00:01:56.036 --> 00:01:57.767
Then he couldn't even
stay up till midnight.

00:01:57.807 --> 00:02:01.978
Set his alarm for seven and planned to
lock himself away. That's not laziness.

00:02:02.018 --> 00:02:05.694
That's devotion. And then
there's Kieran from Manchester.

00:02:05.734 --> 00:02:08.682
He told the BBC he was going
on a pilgrimage to Wales,

00:02:08.722 --> 00:02:10.558
completely off grid for a week.

00:02:10.598 --> 00:02:13.337
His partner, he said, had
been incredibly understanding,

00:02:13.377 --> 00:02:15.826
though he suspected she was
questioning why their romantic

00:02:15.866 --> 00:02:18.749
getaway revolved around a
PlayStation. I love that.

00:02:18.789 --> 00:02:21.798
A week in Wales, not for
the hills, for Vice City.

00:02:21.838 --> 00:02:23.893
And he wasn't the only
one who went all in.

00:02:23.933 --> 00:02:27.120
One couple turned it into a little
holiday at home. He booked the day.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:29.739
She bought him a £90 PlayStation voucher.

00:02:29.779 --> 00:02:32.758
He packed her a getaway
basket: Lego, colouring books,

00:02:32.798 --> 00:02:36.394
pyjamas, so she wouldn't feel left out
while he disappeared into the game.

00:02:36.434 --> 00:02:38.016
That's not a national crisis.

00:02:38.056 --> 00:02:41.757
That's people trying, in their own
slightly chaotic way, to be happy.

00:02:41.797 --> 00:02:44.300
And if you didn't already
have the kit, good luck.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.734
In the weeks before launch, John
Lewis said sales of consoles

00:02:47.774 --> 00:02:50.856
and accessories were up
62% on the year before.

00:02:50.896 --> 00:02:54.251
Currys said PlayStation
5 sales were up 149%.

00:02:55.187 --> 00:02:57.347
People weren't just buying
a game; they were building

00:02:57.387 --> 00:03:01.018
a night they could disappear into. And
then they broke the internet doing it.

00:03:01.058 --> 00:03:05.723
The evening before launch, Virgin Media
O2 smashed its own broadband record.

00:03:05.763 --> 00:03:09.584
BT and EE said they hit their
highest home broadband peak ever.

00:03:09.624 --> 00:03:12.767
One player told the BBC he was
still on it in the early hours,

00:03:12.807 --> 00:03:15.231
even though he had to be
up for work in five hours.

00:03:15.271 --> 00:03:18.234
"Why am I playing a game I
have years to enjoy?" he said.

00:03:18.274 --> 00:03:20.190
Because that's the thing
about a wait this long:

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:24.151
when it finally arrives, five hours'
sleep suddenly feels negotiable.

00:03:24.191 --> 00:03:27.952
Britain didn't queue outside Game at
midnight. There was no disc to queue for.

00:03:27.992 --> 00:03:30.197
Just a download the size of a small moon,

00:03:30.237 --> 00:03:33.360
and a nation sitting in the
dark waiting for a progress bar

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.862
to finish, so they could finally go
somewhere else. Somewhere warmer.

00:03:36.902 --> 00:03:37.920
Somewhere louder.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:41.776
Somewhere that wasn't the group
chat or the bills or the 7am alarm.

00:03:41.816 --> 00:03:46.146
And that, if you ask me, is the real
story. Not that people skipped work.

00:03:46.186 --> 00:03:49.867
That so many of them decided, at the
same time, that this was worth it.

00:03:49.907 --> 00:03:51.568
Which brings me to the Prime Minister.

00:03:51.608 --> 00:03:55.510
Downing Street called the game "a British
success story, made in Edinburgh."

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:57.520
Fair enough, a huge part of it was.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:01.197
So would Andy Burnham be exploring
Vice City with the rest of us?

00:04:01.237 --> 00:04:04.823
A spokesperson said his diary was
very full. He wouldn't be playing it.

00:04:04.863 --> 00:04:07.951
Of course he wouldn't. The rest of the
country had already booked the day.

00:04:07.991 --> 00:04:10.411
And if you think it was only
offices that went quiet,

00:04:10.451 --> 00:04:14.080
wait till you hear about the
schools, because this game is an 18.

